## Final state

**Ad-hoc verification (not a suite run):** `swift test` can't run in this sandbox, so I compiled `Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift` directly and ran a standalone harness. Result: `ALL_PASS` (6/6):

- PASS `hello #world #swift` → `["world", "swift"]` (existing, no regression)
- PASS `just plain text` → `[]` (existing)
- PASS `shopping list,#urgent.` → `["urgent"]` (reported case 1)
- PASS `(#ideas)` → `["ideas"]` (reported case 2)
- PASS `#a, #b; (#c)` → `["a", "b", "c"]` (mixed punctuation)
- PASS `trailing #tag.` → `["tag"]` (trailing punctuation)

The verification script was created under `/private/var/folders/1s/ggz8433s6x56bzrwdtfbblfc0000gn/T/hermes-verify-XXXXXX.sh` (via `mktemp`), executed, and then removed. The temporary run dir and script are cleaned up.

**The fix:** `extractTags(from:)` previously split on whitespace and kept only tokens *starting* with `#`, so it missed tags embedded mid-token (`#urgent` after a comma) and tags with trailing punctuation (`#ideas` inside parens). It now uses the regex `#[\p{L}\p{N}_]+`, which matches a `#` plus any run of letters/numbers/underscores anywhere in the text — stripping surrounding punctuation while preserving document order.

